WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.280
By the early 1990s, only about twenty to

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:06.000
thirty Florida panthers were left, cut off in

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:07.960
the swamps of southern Florida.

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:11.600
Many had kinked tails and poor-quality sperm, and

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:13.480
some had heart defects.

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:16.239
In Texas, pumas of the same species were

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:17.040
doing fine.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:21.840
So in 1995, biologists released eight female Texas

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:23.120
pumas into Florida.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:26.400
Within about a decade, the panther population had

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:27.840
roughly tripled.

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:30.120
What had gone wrong with the Florida panthers'

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:33.040
alleles, and how did eight newcomers help fix

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:33.440
it?

00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:36.626
Start with their gene pools.

00:00:36.907 --> 00:00:39.026
A gene pool is every allele in a

00:00:39.067 --> 00:00:42.586
population, and an allele's frequency is its share

00:00:42.667 --> 00:00:44.506
of all the copies of that gene.

00:00:45.147 --> 00:00:48.906
Each panther carries two copies, so twenty-five panthers

00:00:48.987 --> 00:00:50.266
hold fifty copies.

00:00:50.907 --> 00:00:52.986
If ten of those copies are one allele,

00:00:53.067 --> 00:00:55.386
its frequency is ten out of fifty, or

00:00:55.707 --> 00:00:56.906
zero point two.

00:00:57.227 --> 00:00:59.866
The Florida pool was small and cut off,

00:00:59.987 --> 00:01:02.266
because the pumas that once lived between Florida

00:01:02.307 --> 00:01:04.186
and Texas had been wiped out.

00:01:04.667 --> 00:01:06.666
And it had lost alleles that the Texas

00:01:06.747 --> 00:01:08.026
pool still had.

00:01:08.347 --> 00:01:10.586
So what changes a gene pool?

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:13.800
The first answer is chance.

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:17.880
Each generation, only some animals happen to breed,

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:20.040
and each parent passes on one allele of

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:21.720
each pair by chance.

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:24.760
So the next generation's alleles are a random

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:26.040
sample of this one's.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:29.880
That's genetic drift, a change in allele frequency

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:30.920
by chance.

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:33.320
It's like tossing a coin for each copy

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:34.040
passed on.

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:37.080
Toss it ten times, and seven heads isn't

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:37.800
unusual.

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:40.440
Toss it a thousand times, and you'll land

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:41.480
close to half.

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:44.920
And each generation's frequency becomes the starting point

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.400
for the next, so the changes add up.

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:50.440
So in a small population, drift can make

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:53.480
an allele common, or lose it completely, whether

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:54.840
it helps or not.

00:01:56.067 --> 00:01:58.906
Drift is strongest when numbers crash.

00:01:59.187 --> 00:02:01.986
Hunting and habitat loss squeezed the panthers down

00:02:02.067 --> 00:02:04.306
to a few dozen, a bottleneck.

00:02:04.867 --> 00:02:07.066
The survivors carried only a sample of the

00:02:07.107 --> 00:02:09.746
old gene pool, and with so few animals,

00:02:09.827 --> 00:02:11.506
relatives often bred together.

00:02:12.147 --> 00:02:15.426
That inbreeding made harmful inherited traits more likely

00:02:15.507 --> 00:02:17.586
to show, like kinked tails.

00:02:18.307 --> 00:02:20.146
Drift works the same way when a few

00:02:20.227 --> 00:02:23.506
individuals leave to start a new population, called

00:02:23.667 --> 00:02:24.706
the founder effect.

00:02:25.347 --> 00:02:28.386
Either way, few individuals means the gene pool

00:02:28.467 --> 00:02:29.826
is a random sample.

00:02:31.034 --> 00:02:32.873
Could mutation repair the damage?

00:02:33.434 --> 00:02:36.393
A mutation adds a brand-new allele, but only

00:02:36.434 --> 00:02:38.073
as a single copy.

00:02:38.154 --> 00:02:41.833
In twenty-five panthers, that's one copy in fifty.

00:02:41.914 --> 00:02:44.073
In a population of a million, it's one

00:02:44.234 --> 00:02:45.833
in two million.

00:02:45.954 --> 00:02:48.633
Mutations at any one gene are rare, so

00:02:48.754 --> 00:02:51.353
rebuilding lost variation this way would take a

00:02:51.514 --> 00:02:52.926
very long time.

00:02:52.981 --> 00:02:55.833
And in a small population, drift loses most

00:02:55.914 --> 00:02:57.993
new alleles before they spread.

00:02:58.074 --> 00:03:00.633
Mutation is the source of new alleles, but

00:03:00.714 --> 00:03:02.793
on its own it changes a gene pool

00:03:02.994 --> 00:03:03.513
slowly.

00:03:04.734 --> 00:03:08.093
What helped was gene flow, alleles moving between

00:03:08.214 --> 00:03:11.213
populations when individuals move and breed.

00:03:11.534 --> 00:03:14.333
The Texas females carried alleles the Florida pool

00:03:14.414 --> 00:03:15.373
had lost.

00:03:15.694 --> 00:03:18.493
After they bred with Florida panthers, their kittens

00:03:18.574 --> 00:03:21.853
had more genetic variation, and kinked tails became

00:03:22.014 --> 00:03:22.973
less common.

00:03:23.294 --> 00:03:26.173
The kittens survived better, and the population grew.

00:03:26.974 --> 00:03:29.293
Gene flow didn't make any new alleles.

00:03:29.454 --> 00:03:31.933
It moved existing ones, and it made the

00:03:32.014 --> 00:03:33.693
two gene pools more alike.

00:03:34.334 --> 00:03:37.053
Animals that leave a population take their alleles

00:03:37.174 --> 00:03:37.933
out, too.

00:03:39.167 --> 00:03:41.566
So which process matters most?

00:03:41.807 --> 00:03:43.326
It depends on the population.

00:03:43.807 --> 00:03:47.406
In a small, isolated population, drift usually has

00:03:47.487 --> 00:03:49.086
the biggest short-term effect.

00:03:49.647 --> 00:03:52.846
Where populations swap many migrants, gene flow can

00:03:52.927 --> 00:03:55.966
change frequencies fast, and keep the pools similar.

00:03:56.447 --> 00:04:00.046
And only mutation makes genuinely new alleles, so

00:04:00.087 --> 00:04:02.286
it matters most over long times.

00:04:02.927 --> 00:04:04.646
Selection is different again.

00:04:04.847 --> 00:04:07.726
It changes frequencies for a reason, because some

00:04:07.807 --> 00:04:11.086
alleles help survival or breeding, while drift has

00:04:11.247 --> 00:04:12.206
no direction.

00:04:13.434 --> 00:04:14.233
Two aids.

00:04:14.514 --> 00:04:17.753
Drift is luck, and luck matters more in

00:04:17.834 --> 00:04:18.713
small groups.

00:04:19.274 --> 00:04:21.673
And gene flow tends to make populations more

00:04:21.754 --> 00:04:22.233
alike.

00:04:22.794 --> 00:04:25.033
Drift can pull them apart, and so can

00:04:25.314 --> 00:04:28.473
selection, when their environments favour different alleles.

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:31.340
Two exam questions now.

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:32.340
First.

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:35.780
An island population of five hundred skinks has

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:38.500
allele b at a frequency of zero point

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:39.300
one.

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:42.580
A cyclone kills all but ten skinks, and

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:44.900
survival has nothing to do with this gene.

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:48.540
The ten survivors carry four copies of b.

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:51.860
Calculate the frequency of b after the cyclone.

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:55.300
Then identify the process that changed it, and

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:56.500
justify your answer.

00:04:57.140 --> 00:04:58.420
Pause here to answer.

00:05:08.834 --> 00:05:11.953
Ten skinks carry twenty copies, so the frequency

00:05:12.034 --> 00:05:15.313
is four out of twenty, zero point two,

00:05:15.394 --> 00:05:16.513
double what it was.

00:05:17.154 --> 00:05:19.473
Survival had nothing to do with this gene,

00:05:19.554 --> 00:05:21.553
and only ten survived.

00:05:21.634 --> 00:05:23.793
So this is a bottleneck, a form of

00:05:23.874 --> 00:05:26.993
genetic drift, because the survivors were a small,

00:05:27.194 --> 00:05:28.593
random sample.

00:05:28.634 --> 00:05:31.553
Saying b increased because it helped skinks survive

00:05:31.794 --> 00:05:33.313
is the classic mistake.

00:05:34.534 --> 00:05:35.093
Second.

00:05:35.574 --> 00:05:39.253
A small, isolated population of thirty possums has

00:05:39.334 --> 00:05:41.413
lost much of its genetic variation.

00:05:42.054 --> 00:05:44.373
A conservation team plans to bring in ten

00:05:44.454 --> 00:05:46.773
possums from a large population of the same

00:05:46.854 --> 00:05:47.493
species.

00:05:48.134 --> 00:05:51.173
Evaluate this plan, compared with leaving the population

00:05:51.334 --> 00:05:54.373
isolated and relying on mutation to restore its

00:05:54.454 --> 00:05:55.413
variation.

00:05:55.734 --> 00:05:57.093
Pause here to answer.

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:09.980
Bringing in possums is gene flow.

00:06:10.220 --> 00:06:12.860
It adds many existing alleles at once, so

00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:15.540
variation rises within a generation.

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:18.420
Mutation adds new alleles one copy at a

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:22.220
time, very slowly, and in only thirty possums,

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:23.820
drift would lose most of them.

00:06:24.380 --> 00:06:26.460
So the plan is far better at restoring

00:06:26.540 --> 00:06:29.980
variation, although the added alleles still face drift.

00:06:30.620 --> 00:06:33.100
Thinking gene flow creates new alleles is the

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:34.380
classic mistake.

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:37.440
So when a question shows a gene pool

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:40.520
changing, ask what happened to the alleles.

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:42.320
Did a brand-new allele appear?

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:43.600
That's mutation.

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:47.480
Did alleles arrive or leave with moving individuals?

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:48.880
That's gene flow.

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:51.920
Did frequencies change by chance, especially in a

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:52.720
small group?

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:53.840
That's drift.

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:56.960
Did they change because carriers survive or breed

00:06:57.040 --> 00:06:57.440
better?

00:06:57.760 --> 00:06:58.960
That's selection.

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:02.080
Then judge which matters most from the population's

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:03.600
size and isolation.

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:06.720
Next, what counts as biotechnology.

00:07:07.934 --> 00:07:09.373
Here are the key notes.

00:07:09.614 --> 00:07:11.533
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:11.614 --> 00:07:12.413
to copy them.
